Name: shr-rs
Version: %{VERSION}
Release: 1%{?dist}
Summary: Sliced Hybrid RAID (SHR/SHR-2) manager over mdadm, LVM and Btrfs
License: MIT OR Apache-2.0
URL: https://github.com/heavycaffeiner/shr-rs
Source0: %{name}-%{version}-x86_64.tar.gz

# Source0 carries a statically linked musl binary cross-built by the release
# workflow (.github/workflows/release.yml), not a source tree: this project
# is developed on Windows and has no in-package cargo build. Hence no
# debuginfo subpackage -- there is nothing for rpm to extract it from.
ExclusiveArch: x86_64
%global debug_package %{nil}

BuildRequires: systemd-rpm-macros

# Runtime dependencies are the external commands the engine actually shells
# out to, counted from the source rather than guessed: mdadm, parted,
# lsblk/blkid (util-linux), the lvm2 family, curl (webhook delivery), and
# systemctl/udevadm (systemd).
#
# btrfs-progs and smartmontools are Recommends, NOT Requires. btrfs-progs
# lives in EPEL on both EL9 and EL10, so a hard dependency would make this
# package uninstallable on a stock Rocky/RHEL -- and installing it there would
# not help anyway, because the stock kernel ships no btrfs module either (see
# README's Btrfs note for the kernel-lt route). mdadm and LVM management,
# which is most of what this tool does, works without either.
Requires: mdadm
Requires: lvm2
Requires: parted
Requires: util-linux
Requires: curl
Requires: systemd
Recommends: btrfs-progs
Recommends: smartmontools

%description
shr-rs manages SHR -- Sliced Hybrid RAID -- pools of mixed-capacity disks on
the standard Linux stack. Disks are sliced into offset-aligned bands, each band
becomes its own mdadm array, the arrays are concatenated by LVM, and Btrfs sits
on top -- so disks of differing sizes contribute their full capacity and the
array can be expanded online.

It provides create/expand with journaled rollback and crash resume, scrub
(mdadm check plus Btrfs), disk replacement, SMART monitoring, transparent
zstd recompression, snapshots with retention, an adaptive reshape throttle,
and per-group systemd timers. Run it with no arguments for the interactive
TUI, or with a subcommand for the scriptable CLI (add --json for
schema-versioned machine-readable output).

Install cockpit-shr-rs for the web dashboard.

%prep
%autosetup -n %{name}-%{version}

%build
# Nothing to build: Source0 is the release binary. See the comment on Source0.

%install
install -Dpm0755 shr-rs %{buildroot}%{_bindir}/shr-rs
install -Dpm0644 packaging/shr-rs.service %{buildroot}%{_unitdir}/shr-rs.service
install -dm0700 %{buildroot}%{_sharedstatedir}/shr-rs
install -dm0755 %{buildroot}%{_sysconfdir}/shr-rs

%check
# Smoke-test the packaged artifact after rpm has stripped it. --version takes
# the CLI path unconditionally (detect_ui_mode: any argument that is neither
# --tui nor --no-tui means CLI), so this does not depend on a terminal.
%{buildroot}%{_bindir}/shr-rs --version

# shr-rs.service is a state tailer and is deliberately NOT enabled by default;
# see the comment at the top of the unit. %%systemd_post only applies presets,
# so shipping it stays inert unless an operator or a preset enables it. The
# timers that do the real periodic work are created at runtime by
# `shr-rs schedule install` and are not owned by this package.
%post
%systemd_post shr-rs.service

%preun
%systemd_preun shr-rs.service

%postun
%systemd_postun_with_restart shr-rs.service

%files
%license LICENSE-MIT LICENSE-APACHE THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt
%doc README.md
%{_bindir}/shr-rs
%{_unitdir}/shr-rs.service
%dir %attr(0700,root,root) %{_sharedstatedir}/shr-rs
%dir %{_sysconfdir}/shr-rs

%changelog
